  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_030 (scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, concat) at 0.632. Multiprofile + avgpool + concat + scvi_n200 are all supported by the data (multiprofile >> singleprofile, avgpool > transformer, concat ≈ weighted, scvi > pca). However, the image encoder has only been tested as resnet50 in the multiprofile multimodal setting; imagenet_vit_l is completely untested. Since the image modality clearly contributes (multimodal 0.632 vs rna_only 0.591), the quality of the image representation is a major unexplored design axis. Question: Does a stronger, ViT-based image encoder (imagenet_vit_l) improve over resnet50 when all other settings are held at their known-best values? Why this is the best next experiment: It is a clean single-variable extension of the current best (config_030 → config_036, only image encoder changes), making the result directly interpretable. If imagenet_vit_l helps, it opens a high-ceiling branch; if it doesn't, we efficiently rule out that entire sub-space. No other untested dimension (scgpt encoder, weighted fusion) has as much potential to shift performance given the evidence so far."
}

